-- title:
--   Corollary 1.8.4 (Chow, 1948): the centre of $B_n$ is infinite cyclic
-- statement:
--   **Corollary 1.8.4 (Chow, 1948).** If $n \ge 3$, the centre of the braid group $B_n$ is the
--   infinite cyclic subgroup generated by
--
--   $$\bigl(\sigma_1\sigma_2\cdots\sigma_{n-1}\bigr)^n .$$
--
--   The statement formalized here has two parts: the centre of $B_n$ equals the subgroup of all
--   integral powers of that element, and the element has infinite order — together, that the centre
--   is infinite cyclic with the stated generator. The hypothesis $n \ge 3$ cannot be dropped: $B_2$
--   is infinite cyclic and therefore abelian, so its centre is all of $B_2$ and is strictly larger
--   than the subgroup generated by $\sigma_1^2$.
--
--   The alternative description of the generator as the pure braid
--   $(A_{12})(A_{13}A_{23})\cdots(A_{1n}A_{2n}\cdots A_{n-1,n})$ given in the book is not part of this
--   statement, since the generators $A_{rs}$ are not introduced in the formal development.
-- source:
--   Joan S. Birman, *Braids, Links, and Mapping Class Groups*, Annals of Mathematics Studies 82, Princeton University Press, 1974, Chapter 1, p. 28, Corollary 1.8.4 [Chow, 1948]

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_BraidsLinksMCG_ArtinBraidGroup

namespace BraidsLinksMCG

theorem cor_1_8_4_center_braid_group (n : ℕ) (hn : 3 ≤ n) :
    Subgroup.center (ArtinBraidGroup n) = Subgroup.zpowers (sigmaProd n ^ n) ∧
      ¬ IsOfFinOrder (sigmaProd n ^ n) := by sorry

end BraidsLinksMCG
